You’re right—I underweighted them. Security, compliance, and regulatory judgment are core AI architecture expectations, especially for systems handling personal data or making consequential decisions. Current architect postings explicitly ask for security and privacy controls, governance, auditability, and work with legal and risk teams. CI&T, CFGI.

The market expects an architect to do three things:
- Secure the system: design access controls for data and tools, limit what agents can do, protect sensitive information, and test threats such as prompt injection. These are application architecture decisions, not just model settings. OWASP LLM Top 10.
- Turn compliance requirements into working controls: specify what must be logged, evaluated, reviewed by a human, monitored, and documented. The architecture must produce evidence that those controls operate. NIST Generative AI Profile.
- Identify regulatory obligations early: establish the system’s intended use, jurisdiction, and the organisation’s role before choosing its design. In the EU, AI Act obligations vary by risk category and by whether an organisation provides or deploys the system; GDPR may also require a data protection impact assessment for high-risk processing of personal data. EU AI Act Service Desk, European Commission GDPR guidance.
The architect should own how these requirements shape the system and its delivery. Legal, privacy, security, and regulatory specialists determine and review the applicable obligations with them. In Spain, this is a substantial part of the role: EU AI Act rules are applying in stages, with transparency obligations already applicable as of August 2026 and some high-risk rules scheduled later. EU AI Act implementation timeline.





