what about memory, long task execution...are they inside those groups?

Yes, but I buried two important categories. I’d give them their own rows:
Tool type	Core job	Where it overlaps
Memory	Preserve useful facts, decisions, and task state across sessions; retrieve the right information later; correct or forget stale information.	Data connectors, permissions, evaluation
Long task execution	Run work over hours or days with checkpoints, retries, budgets, approvals, and a reliable way to resume after interruption.	Deployment, monitoring, security

The distinction matters. Memory answers “what should the system remember?” Long task execution answers “how does it keep working reliably?” A model can have excellent memory and still lose a task when a process stops; an executor can resume perfectly while carrying the wrong or outdated context.

I’d put both among the most relevant AI tool opportunities, especially for agents expected to do sustained work rather than answer a single prompt.

is that any other important point you missed?

Yes. I focused too much on tools around the model and missed parts of the agent’s working system:
Missing category	Why it matters
Planning and coordination	Break a goal into steps, choose what to do next, and manage dependencies between tasks or agents. Long task execution keeps work running; planning decides what work to run.
Action tools	Let an agent make changes in software or other systems, then verify the result and undo mistakes where possible. A data connector that only reads is insufficient for many tasks.
Human control	Show progress and decisions clearly so a person can correct direction or approve consequential actions at the right time.
Learning from feedback	Use failures and user corrections to improve future runs. This goes beyond storing them in memory.

So my revised picture is: memory, planning, durable execution, action, verification, and human control form the core of an agent that can complete substantial work. Data access, security, deployment, and cost tools support that core. This is a design judgment about where reusable tools are needed, not a measured ranking of market size.

