what type of tools companies are looking for to solve their "AIlization"?

If by “AIlization” you mean bringing AI into a company’s everyday work, companies are looking for tools that solve a specific business problem and can be trusted in production. In McKinsey’s 2026 survey, 80% of respondents said AI improved individual productivity, but only 37% reported a positive impact on company earnings. That gap explains much of the demand.
McKinsey
Tool type	What the company needs it to do
Workflow tools	Complete a defined task, such as handling support tickets, processing invoices, preparing proposals, or updating CRM records.
Data and integration tools	Connect AI to company documents and systems while respecting existing access permissions.
Governance and security tools	Control what AI can see and do, require approval for sensitive actions, and keep an audit trail.
Testing and monitoring tools	Measure answer quality, catch failures, and monitor agents after launch.
Adoption and ROI tools	Show which teams use AI and whether it saves time, improves quality, or increases revenue.
Training tools	Help staff use AI effectively within their actual roles and processes.

The strongest opportunity is often a specific workflow with a measurable result: for example, “reduce the time to resolve a support case” rather than “an AI assistant for everything.” McKinsey finds that companies seeing the most value redesign workflows around AI. Security and risk are also major buying concerns, especially when an AI agent can take actions in other systems.
